quite frankly I'm tired of seeing white  women taking up all of the space in a  movement that was inherently black and  indigenous my name is Dominic Drake Ford  and I work very diligently to be zero  waste ish some exciting things that I do  on a day-to-day would be carrying my  mason jar my water bottle I bring my own  tote bag whenever I need to go grocery  shopping I try to reduce single-use  plastic I try to use tin and glass as  much as possible in my home I just  started my first backyard garden so I'm  growing my own food I have my own  compost bin so I'm composting waste  that's in the home I DIY as much as I  can I upcycle and repurpose clothing  when I can I vintage shop thrift shop  sometimes I make my own house cleaning  products sometimes I make my own skin  care and hair products and one of my  favorite things to do is make my own  toothpaste and another way that I really  work to be sustainable on a day-to-day  is showing that on my insta stories my  sustainability journeys didn't happen  until I moved to New York and I was  attending this colloquium and it was  about sustainability and  environmentalism and I really looked and  saw that there was nobody nobody who  looked like me and the only person I saw  that looked remotely like me was a  laborer being shown on a screen and so I  was like you know what I'm not with this  this is where a shift needs to happen  and this is where I need to develop a  voice so that we can create change in  terms of formal education I have my  bachelor's degree in business  environmental management and then I have  my master's degree in sustainable  entrepreneurship in fashion growing up  in Oakland I definitely had access to  nature and so that was where my first  love was sort of cultivated one of the  reasons why I refrain from wearing the  zero waste title the zero waste movement  felt very exclusive it had sort of a  privileged veil over it and the  conversations that were taking place in  the zero-waste community never paid  homage to black and indigenous cultures  who sort of created that mandala to be  zero ways or to be resourceful being  resourceful has been something that  communities of color have had to do for  generations and so being able to take  that first step to shift our  understanding it's super important so I  want to see more black and indigenous  people being highlighted in the  zero-waste community quite frankly I'm  tired of seeing white women taking up  all of the space in a movement that was  inherently black and indigenous and the  environmental crisis is  disproportionately affecting black and  indigenous people despite the fact that  they've had these zero-waste manifestos  always I am the co-creator of an  initiative called sustainable Brooklyn  which bridges the gap between the  sustainability movement and targeted  communities we are two black women who  came together realizing that number one  we were tired of the narrative of  sustainability again being white driven  but also we were tired of going to the  sustainability events and not being  represented in the audience not being  represented on the panel not being  represented as models not being  represented in creative direction and  most importantly sustainability not  discussing solutions for black and  indigenous communities on the ground  we're able to create events and provide  resources and discuss sustainability  mainly fashion food and wellness but  discuss it from such a holistic approach  in a way that has never been done before  our last symposium is called Earth Hour  next symposium is called air and air is  gonna obviously focus on air quality but  also really look at the mental health  aspect of breathing and breath work and  then fire and water are going to take  place in 2020  so we're super excited to be able to  have this elemental series that's going  to transcend how we look and understand  and sort of activate on sustainability  my baby is melanin s and the S stands  for and sustainable style melanin s is a  digital space that celebrates a black  and indigenous communities of color in  fashion food wellness and land  sovereignty and it's a unique space  where I'm able to interview all of these  different trailblazers all of these  different vanguards from around the  globe who either have a brand have a  business or they have an awesome  sustainability initiative I think now  more than ever it's imperative for us to  redefine sustainability and give it a  very clear historical context but also  make sure we're including a very  authentic cultural lens to that mix and  really making sure that we are giving  agency and stakeholder ship to black and  indigenous communities if we really want  to have a sustainability movement  [Music]
 

